<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    nexus-tk070.p6a (RDR-194 § D5, § P6a) — nexus.memory.ttl -> ttl_days,
    with CHECK (ttl_days IS NULL OR ttl_days > 0).

    WHY. NULL already means "permanent" for this column, but 0 was also
    reachable and meant the OPPOSITE: MemoryRepository.expire() (formerly
    MemoryRepository.java:566-573,584, now the ttl_days-bound copy of the
    same logic) filters WHERE ttl_days IS NOT NULL and computes
    effective_ttl = ttl_days * (1 + ln(access_count + 1)), so a stored 0
    yields effective_ttl = 0 and the row is deleted by the very next sweep
    — the exact opposite of "permanent". Only NULL=permanent can be defended
    by a CHECK (D5, A12): under a 0=permanent convention, 0 is both a legal
    integer TTL and a sentinel, and no CHECK can distinguish "no expiry"
    from "zero days". The rename to ttl_days (A14) documents the unit that
    made 0-as-sentinel plausible in the first place; all four sibling TTL
    columns across the schema are integer days.

    Historically the trap was papered over by exactly ONE caller
    (src/nexus/mcp/core.py's memory_put MCP tool, `ttl=ttl if ttl > 0 else
    None`) while the store itself, and every other caller of
    POST /v1/memory/put, stayed trapped — this is the commit that retires
    that shim and makes the documented "NULL=permanent" contract true at
    the store for EVERY caller (see MemoryHandler.java's now-deleted
    coercePermanentTtl and its replacement boundary validation, same
    commit / same bead).

    COUNTED DELETE, RAISE NOTICE'd (D0.4 / migration-002-tenant-pk.xml and
    fk-004-chunks-collection-registry.xml's fk-004-0-reconcile-precount
    precedent). A ttl=0 row existing at migration time is UNREPRESENTABLE
    under the new CHECK — it must be deleted, not silently retained with a
    dropped constraint or silently reinterpreted. Per nexus-tk070.cc6
    (cloud-count-6, T2-recorded gate precondition for this bead): nexus.memory
    ttl=0 -> 0 rows in the counted population this migration runs against.
    The DELETE + RAISE NOTICE ships regardless of that measured zero — the
    count is evidence of what THIS box holds at migration time, not a
    structurally-guaranteed zero (identical reasoning to fk-004-0-reconcile-
    precount's own header).

    RLS TOGGLE on the DELETE. nexus.memory carries FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY
    (memory-001-baseline.xml, changeset 3). Liquibase runs as nexus_admin,
    the table owner but NOT BYPASSRLS — an untoggled DELETE under FORCE RLS
    with no nexus.tenant GUC set at migration time would silently delete
    ZERO rows regardless of actual content (the tenant_isolation policy's
    USING predicate never matches a NULL GUC), which would make the RAISE
    NOTICE always report 0 by construction — the exact silent-vacuity trap
    migration-002-tenant-pk.xml's and fk-004-0-reconcile-precount's headers
    document for their own reads/deletes. Toggled NO FORCE for the DELETE,
    restored to FORCE immediately after (minimal window, same shape as
    fk-004-1-reconcile).

    RENAME COLUMN + ADD CHECK are ordinary DDL — unaffected by RLS,
    unconditional on any prior schema state (memory-001-baseline.xml always
    runs before this file in every full walk, so "ttl" always exists here;
    this is ordinary schema evolution, not a dead-object drop that needs
    existence guards like migration-002-tenant-pk.xml's DROP TABLE).

    WIRE CONTRACT (D0.8): this rename is INVISIBLE on the wire.
    POST/GET /v1/memory/* still read/write the JSON key "ttl" — only the
    Postgres column and the jOOQ-generated Java field/getter (MEMORY.TTL ->
    MEMORY.TTL_DAYS, MemoryRecord.getTtl() -> .getTtlDays()) move.
    MemoryHandler's recordToMap keeps `m.put("ttl", r.getTtlDays())`.

    INDEX NAMES UNCHANGED. idx_memory_tenant_ttl_timestamp still carries its
    original name after the RENAME COLUMN — Postgres tracks index column
    references by attnum, not name, so the index transparently follows the
    rename with no ALTER INDEX needed. Renaming the index itself is outside
    this changeset's scope (column rename only, D0.8).

    D0.6 ROLLBACK: drops the CHECK, renames ttl_days back to ttl. Whatever
    rows this changeset's DELETE removed are UNRECOVERABLE — stated inline,
    not silently omitted, matching legacy-001-drop-chash-alias.xml's and
    migration-002-tenant-pk.xml's identical rollback discipline.

    D0.1 placement: changelog TAIL, immediately after migration-002-tenant-
    pk.xml (the current tail) and before the grants-nexus-svc.xml runAlways
    block, matching the established late-append convention. Paired with
    plans-003-ttl-days.xml (same bead, same shape, sibling table) —
    EXPECT_NEW_CHANGESETS=2 for this bead's two files together, one
    changeset each.

    CLIENT PAIRING (fix-pass 2026-08-20, substantive-critic ship-blocker):
    this migration ships together with the MemoryHandler boundary-400 change
    and the MCP core.py coercion deletion in the SAME commit (nexus-tk070.p6a)
    — the engine half (this file + plans-003-ttl-days.xml + MemoryHandler's
    requirePositiveOrNullTtl) is "inert or wrong against any earlier engine"
    per RDR-194 D5's own Delivery section, and must ship paired with a client
    release whose REQUIRED_ENGINE_VERSION carries these CHECKs (the RDR-194
    P7 paired cut) — docs/wire-contract-pending.md's ## Unshipped section
    declares this pairing, entry added in the immediately-following commit
    once THIS commit's own sha is known (a commit's tree cannot contain its
    own resulting hash — the established two-commit pattern this repo
    already uses, e.g. 2c95bae17 declaring 3b2901141, 433246839 declaring
    f1c669b47). Bounded blast radius in the interim (verified, not asserted):
    an engine that predates this commit still carries MemoryHandler's now-
    retired coercePermanentTtl, so a client already carrying this commit's
    core.py talking to an un-paired old engine still gets the PRE-EXISTING
    silent-coerce-to-NULL behavior — unchanged from before this bead, not a
    new footgun.
-->
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.4.xsd">

    <!-- ── Changeset memory-003-1: counted DELETE, RENAME, ADD CHECK ────────── -->
    <changeSet id="memory-003-1" author="nexus-tk070.p6a">
        <comment>
            nexus.memory.ttl -> ttl_days, CHECK (ttl_days IS NULL OR ttl_days > 0).
            Counted DELETE of pre-existing ttl=0 rows (RAISE NOTICE'd, RLS
            toggle-wrapped so FORCE RLS cannot hide rows from the count) —
            those rows were already effectively dead (MemoryRepository.expire()
            yields effective_ttl=0 for ttl=0, swept on the next run). NULL
            remains the sole permanent sentinel; 0 becomes unrepresentable.
        </comment>
        <sql splitStatements="false" stripComments="false">
ALTER TABLE nexus.memory NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

DO $$
DECLARE
    v_deleted_count BIGINT;
BEGIN
    DELETE FROM nexus.memory WHERE ttl = 0;
    GET DIAGNOSTICS v_deleted_count = ROW_COUNT;
    RAISE NOTICE 'nexus-tk070.p6a: deleted % nexus.memory row(s) with ttl = 0 — unrepresentable under the new CHECK (ttl_days IS NULL OR ttl_days > 0); these rows were already effectively dead (MemoryRepository.expire() computes effective_ttl = ttl * (1 + ln(access_count + 1)), so ttl=0 yields effective_ttl=0 and the row is swept on the very next expire() run) — deletion is unrecoverable', v_deleted_count;
END $$;

ALTER TABLE nexus.memory FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

ALTER TABLE nexus.memory RENAME COLUMN ttl TO ttl_days;

ALTER TABLE nexus.memory
    ADD CONSTRAINT memory_ttl_days_positive_chk CHECK (ttl_days IS NULL OR ttl_days > 0);
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <!-- D0.6: restores the baseline shape exactly (drop CHECK, rename
                 back). Rows this changeset's DELETE removed are UNRECOVERABLE
                 — this rollback restores the SCHEMA, not the deleted data,
                 matching migration-002-tenant-pk.xml's / legacy-001-drop-
                 chash-alias.xml's identical rollback discipline. -->
            <sql splitStatements="true" stripComments="false">
ALTER TABLE nexus.memory DROP CONSTRAINT IF EXISTS memory_ttl_days_positive_chk;
ALTER TABLE nexus.memory RENAME COLUMN ttl_days TO ttl;
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
